<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Shewa</h1><p class="st-meta">1682 CE – 1889 CE (approx.) · capital Ankober</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Amhara kingdom in central Ethiopia that rose to virtual independence and became the power base of Menelik II, the founder of modern Ethiopia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>While the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> emperors weakened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negasi-krestos">Negasi Krestos</a>, a Shewan noble claiming Solomonic descent, established an autonomous lordship in northern Shewa around 1682. His successors, at first with the title meridazmach, gradually extended their rule over the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> districts, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankober">Ankober</a> and later Angolalla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian kingship with pragmatic alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and Muslim neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a> (r. 1813 to 1847), who took the title negus, built a wealthy state and received British and French missions. Shewa controlled trade routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibe-river">Gibe</a> region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> to the coast at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-tadjoura">Tadjoura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1855 to 1856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> conquered Shewa, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie's</a> grandson, escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdala">Magdala</a> and restored the kingdom in 1865. When Menelik became emperor in 1889, Shewa merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, with its new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-shewa.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Shewa" width="320" height="301"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Sahla_Sellase_from_Shewa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1682 CE – 1889 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ankober</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Negasi Krestos, Sahle Selassie, Menelik II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amharic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shewa, Shoa, Kingdom of Shoa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=kingdom-of-shewa" aria-label="Kingdom of Shewa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5781 1820 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Shewa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6101 2076L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6101 2076h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6101 2076h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ankober. <a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=kingdom-of-shewa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
